A||||2||Cliff D.||need fuel pressure help||||1||
Z||000000||Cliff D.||05-08-2001||02:35 PM||||So I have problems with cold starts because my fuel pressure goes to zero when I shut down. First of all the fuel pump is fine. Now when I pinch the pressure hose, the pressure quickly goes to 10lbs and holds steady. When I release the vise-grips, it goes to 0. When I pinch the return line, it does not hold pressure at all. If I followed the flowchart in the manual correctly, I need to "find the leaking injector and fix it... also inspect cold start valve" Does anyone have any input on this, and what is the cost/benefit ratio of dismantling down to the valve covers to clean/replace rockers and pushrods and maybe scrape some crud off the springs? thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||05-08-2001||07:05 PM||||Well, logically, it <B>does</B> sound like a leaking injector to me.  If my memory serves, you can get the fuel rail+ injectors off by after you remove the upper plenum.  And, if you're at all lucky, you won't even need new gaskets.<P>Personally, I would pull the fuel rail, put rags on the injectors, run fuel pump, locate the faulty injector(s) (by looking at their deposits on the rags), and fix/replace the injector.  Then, put it all back together and see if that cures the original complaint.<P>You can do the valve cover removal / spring cleaning  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> later, if you have time.      ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff D.||05-09-2001||02:46 PM||||thanks, I'll do just that. I have the WHOLE top end off my other motor and remember how many little things needed to be dealt with for dissassembly. If I can get into the engine compartment (no battery and lost keys) where all the pieces are from that car, I will hopefully find a good injector on that rail. I'll save a gazillion dollars and just get new o-rings.||||reg||1
